Mentalists make it feel like you had a free choice and they somehow knew what was in your head. The trick usually comes from when they learned the answer, how much freedom you actually had, or what you remember happening.

Method	How it works
Forcing a choice	You feel like you picked freely, but the procedure steers you toward a known outcome. With magician’s choice, “pick two” can mean “keep those two” or “eliminate those two,” depending on what you touch.
Secretly getting the answer	You write down a name and fold the paper. The performer gets a quick peek or uses a prop that captures the writing. They can reveal it minutes later, when the writing feels like ancient history.
Multiple outs	The performer prepares for several outcomes and reveals only the prediction that matches yours. You see one envelope opened, and that makes it feel like there was only ever one prediction.
Cold reading	The performer starts with plausible guesses and uses your words and reactions to narrow them. You may remember the final hit more vividly than the clues you gave along the way.
Hot reading	The performer knows a detail in advance, sometimes from a casual conversation before the show. When it comes up onstage, it feels like they just figured it out.

Here’s a little version you can feel. Imagine three coins on a table, and I want you to end up with the middle one. I say, “Put your hand on a coin.” If you touch the middle coin, I say, “Great, slide it toward you.” If you touch an outside coin, I say, “Great, slide it out of the way.” Same opening line, different meaning assigned after you act. You made a real choice. I controlled what that choice did.

That’s the part I think you’d enjoy for Pantheon. The audience only experiences one branch. The performer sees the other branches and can make the one you took feel like it was inevitable.